\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sl-259\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \b\fs28\lang2057 {The Jug of Punch 
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057 {
\par One pleasant evening in the month of June, 
\par As I was sitting with my glass and spoon, 
\par A small bird sat on an ivy bush, 
\par And the song he sang was the Jug of Punch. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus: 
\par Too-ra loo-ra loo, too-ra loo-ra loo, 
\par Too-ra loo-ra loo, too-ra loo-ra loo. 
\par A small bird sat on an ivy bush, 
\par And the song it sang was the Jug of Punch. 
\par }{
\par What more diversion can a man desire, 
\par Than to be seated by a snug coal fire}{\b , 
\par }{Upon his knee a pretty wench 
\par Aye and on the table a jug of punch. 
\par 
\par If I were sick and very bad, 
\par And was not able to go or stand, 
\par I would not think it all amiss, 
\par To pledge my shoes for a jug of punch. 
\par 
\par The doctor fails with all his art, 
\par To cure an impression on the heart. 
\par But if life was gone, within an inch, 
\par What would bring it back but a jug of punch. 
\par 
\par And when I\rquote m dead and in my grave 
\par No costly tombstone will I crave, 
\par Just lay me down on my native peat 
\par With a jug of punch at my head and feet. 
\par 
\par 
\par \sect }\sectd \psz9\linex0\sectdefaultcl \pard\plain \s16\li1440\sl-259\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \b\fs28\lang2057 {The Holy Ground 
\par }\pard\plain \li1440\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057 {
\par Adieu my fair young maiden, ten thousand times adieu. 
\par We are bound away from the Holy Ground and the girls we love so true. 
\par We\rquote ll sail the salt seas over and we\rquote ll return once more, 
\par And still I live in hope to see the Holy Ground once more. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par (Shouted) Fine girl you are! 
\par (Sung) You\rquote re the girl that I adore, 
\par And still I live in hope to see the Holy Ground once more, 
\par }{
\par Now when we\rquote re out a-sailing and you are far behind 
\par Fine letters will I write to you with the secrets of my mind, 
\par The secrets of my mind, my girl, you\rquote re the girl that I adore, 
\par And still I live in hope to see the Holy Ground once more. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par Oh now the storm is raging and we are far from shore; 
\par The poor old ship she\rquote s sinking fast and the riggings they are tore 
\par The night is dark and dreary, we can scarcely see the moon, 
\par But still I live in hope to see the Holy Ground once more. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par It\rquote s now the storm is over and we are safe on shore 
\par We\rquote ll drink a toast to the Holy Ground and the girls that we adore. 
\par We\rquote ll drink strong ale and porter and we\rquote ll make the taproom roar, 
\par And when our money is all spent we\rquote ll go to sea once more. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\li1440\sl-259\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \b\fs28\lang2057 {Maids When You\rquote re Young Never Wed an Old}{\b0  }{Man 
\par }\pard\plain \li1440\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057 {
\par An old man came courting me, hey ding doorum down, 
\par An old man came courting me, me being young, 
\par An old man came courting me, all for to marry me, 
\par Maids when you\rquote re young never wed an old man. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus: 
\par For he\rquote s got no faloorum, fadidledo doorum, 
\par For he\rquote s got no faloorum, fadidle leday, 
\par He\rquote s got no faloorum, he\rquote s lost his ding-doorum, 
\par So maids when you\rquote re young, never wed an old man. 
\par }{
\par Now when we went to the church, hey ding doorum down, 
\par When we went to the church, me being young, 
\par When we went to the church, he left me in the lurch, 
\par Maids when you\rquote re young, never wed an old man. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par Now when we went to our bed, hey ding doorum down, 
\par Now when we went to our bed, me being young, 
\par When we went to our bed, he lay like he was dead, 
\par Maids when you\rquote re young never wed an old man. 
\par \page }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par Threw me leg over him, hey ding doorum down, 
\par Threw me leg over him, me being young, 
\par Threw me leg over him, damn nearly smothered him, 
\par Maids when you\rquote re young never wed an old man. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par Now when he went to sleep, hey ding doorum down, 
\par Now when we went to sleep, me being young, 
\par When he went to sleep, out of bed I did creep, 
\par Into the arms of a handsome young man. 
\par 
\par }{\i And I found his faloorum, fadidle dodoorum,  
\par I found his faloorum, fadidle all day, 
\par I found his faloorum and he got my ding-doorum, 
\par So maids when you\rquote re young never wed an old man. 
\par }{
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\li1440\sl-259\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \b\fs28\lang2057 {The Merry Ploughboy }{\i [Dublin in the Green]}{ 
\par }\pard\plain \li1440\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057 {
\par I am a merry ploughboy and I plough the fields all day, 
\par Till a sudden thought came to my head that I should roam away, 
\par For I am sick and tired of slavery since the day that I was born, 
\par And I\rquote m off to join the I.R.A and I\rquote m off tomorrow morn. 
\par 
\par }{\i And we\rquote re all off to Dublin in the green, in the green, 
\par Where the helmets glisten in the sun, 
\par Where the bayonets flash and the rifles crash, 
\par To the rattle of a Thompson gun. 
\par }{
\par I\rquote ll leave aside me pick and spade, I\rquote ll leave aside me plough, 
\par I\rquote ll leave aside me horse and yoke, I no longer need them now. 
\par I\rquote ll leave aside me Mary, she\rquote s the girl that I adore, 
\par And I wonder if she\rquote ll think of me when she hears the rifles roar. 
\par 
\par }{\i And we\rquote re all off to Dublin in the green, in the green, 
\par Where the helmets glisten in the sun, 
\par Where the bayonets flash and the rifles crash, 
\par To the rattle of a Thompson gun. 
\par }{
\par And when the war is over, and dear old Ireland is free, 
\par I\rquote ll take her to the church to wed and a rebel\rquote s wife she\rquote ll be. 
\par Well some}{\b  }{men fight for silver and some men fight for gold, 
\par But the I.R.A. are fighting for the land that the Saxons stole. 
\par 
\par }{\i And we\rquote re all off to Dublin in the green, in the green, 
\par Where the helmets glisten in the sun, 
\par Where the bayonets flash and the rifles crash, 
\par To the rattle of a Thompson gun. 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
\par \sect }\sectd \psz9\linex0\cols2\colsx288\linebetcol\sectdefaultcl \pard\plain \s16\sl-259\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \b\fs28\lang2057 {The Manchester Rambler 
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057 {
\par 1. I\rquote ve been over Snowdon 
\par I\rquote ve slept up on Crowdon, 
\par I\rquote ve camped by the Wain Stones as well 
\par I\rquote ve sunbathed on Kinder, 
\par Been burnt to a cinder, 
\par And many more things I could tell. 
\par My rucksac has oft been my pillow, 
\par The heather has oft been my bed, 
\par And sooner than part from the mountains 
\par I think I would rather be dead. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus: 
\par I\rquote m a rambler, I\rquote m a rambler from Manchester way, 
\par I get all me pleasure the hard Northern way, 
\par I may be a wage slave on Monday, 
\par But I am a free man on Sunday. 
\par }{
\par 2. The day was just ending 
\par As I was descending, 
\par By Grimesbrook just by Upper Tor, 
\par When a voice cried \ldblquote Hey you\rdblquote  
\par In a way Keepers do, 
\par He\rquote d the worst face that I ever saw. 
\par The things that he said were unpleasant 
\par In the teeth of his fury I said, 
\par Sooner than part from the mountains 
\par I think I would rather be dead. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par 3. Well he called me a louse, 
\par And he said \ldblquote Mind the grouse\rdblquote , 
\par And I tried but still couldn\rquote t see. 
\par Why on Kinder Scout, 
\par And the moors round about, 
\par Wasn\rquote t room for the poor grouse and me. 
\par He said all these lands were his master\rquote s 
\par At this I stood shaking my head, 
\par No man has a claim to the mountains 
\par No more than the deep ocean bed. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par 4. I once loved a maid, 
\par A spot-welder by trade, 
\par She was fair as the rowan in bloom; 
\par And the blue of her eye, 
\par Matched the June moorland sky, 
\par And I wooed her from April to June. 
\par On the day that I should have been married, 
\par I went for a ramble instead 
\par For sooner than part from the mountains 
\par I think I would rather be dead. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par \column 5. There\rquote s pleasure in dragging 
\par Through peat bogs and bragging 
\par Of all kinds of walks that you know. 
\par There\rquote s even a measure 
\par Of some kind of pleasure 
\par In wadding through three feet of snow. 
\par I\rquote ve stood on the edge of the down-fall 
\par And seen all the valley outspread, 
\par And sooner than part from the mountains 
\par I think I would rather be dead. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par 6. So I walk where I will, 
\par Over mountain and hill, 
\par And I lie where the bracken is deep, 
\par I belong to the mountains, 
\par The clear-running Mountains 
\par Where the grey rocks rise rugged and steep. 
\par I\rquote ve seen the white hare in the gully 
\par And the curlew fly high overhead, 
\par And sooner than part from the mountains 
\par I think I would rather be dead. 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sl-259\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \b\fs28\lang2057 {The Leaving of Liverpool}{\b0  
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057 {
\par Farewell to you, my own true love; 
\par I am going far away. 
\par I am bound for California, 
\par But I know that I\rquote ll return some day. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus: 
\par So fare thee well, my own true love, 
\par And when I return, united we will be. 
\par It\rquote s not the leaving of Liverpool that grieves me, 
\par But, my darling, when I think of thee. 
\par }{
\par I have shipped on a Yankee sailing ship; 
\par Davy Crockett is her name. 
\par And Burgess is the captain of her, 
\par And they say she is a floating shame. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par I\rquote ve sailed with Burgess once before, 
\par I think I know him well 
\par If a man\rquote s a sailor he\rquote ll get along, 
\par But if not he\rquote s sure in Hell. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par Oh, the sun is on the harbour, love, 
\par And I wish I could remain, 
\par For I know it will be some long time 
\par Before I see you again. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
