\par \sect }\sectd \psz9\linex0\sectdefaultcl \pard\plain \s16\li1440\sl-259\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \b\fs28\lang2057 {Rawtenstall Annual Fair 
\par }\pard\plain \li1440\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057 {
\par Down behind the gasworks, down in Rawtenstall, 
\par That\rquote s a little town in Lancashire. 
\par Last Saturday night, me and the lads, 
\par Ee by gum we \lquote ad some reet good fun. 
\par There were ice creams, switch backs, coconuts, and waxworks, 
\par Figure-eights and roundabouts, 
\par And weren\rquote t we all delighted when we heard the showman shout: 
\par 
\par \ldblquote Oh, roll up, roll up, come and see the fat girl, 
\par Forty stone o\rquote  loveliness and evr\rquote y bit her own.\rdblquote  
\par Ee she were a big \lquote un, Wi\rquote t accent on the big, 
\par And all the fellas wi\rquote  walking sticks kept giving her a dig. 
\par She were a reet big lassie, we didn\rquote t know her chassis 
\par Were blown up wi\rquote  air I do declare. 
\par Well everthin\rquote  were champion, until some silly clown, 
\par Stabbed her with a pin - said the showman with a frown 
\par \ldblquote All hands to the pumps lads, mi vessel\rquote s goin\rquote  down\rdblquote , 
\par At the Rawtenstall Annual Fair. 
\par 
\par \ldblquote Roll up, roll up, come and see the mermaid, 
\par See the lovely lady, half a woman, half a fish.\rdblquote  
\par In went the lads, to show it wasn\rquote t swank, 
\par When little Tommy Higgins put some whisky in the tank. 
\par Well, she got frisky, swimmin\rquote  in the whisky, 
\par And when she came up for air, 
\par She bowed to the audience, gave her tail a swish, 
\par Her tail it come off and she really looked delish. 
\par She said \ldblquote Now what\rquote s your fancy lads, a bit o\rquote meat or fish?\rdblquote , 
\par At the Rawtenstall Annual Fair. 
\par 
\par \ldblquote Roll up, roll up, see the house o\rquote myst\rquote ry, 
\par Ladies pay a tanner to be tickled in the dark.\rdblquote  
\par In went the lads, just for a peep, 
\par The showman pulled a lever and they landed in a heap. 
\par Well, down he shot \lquote em, right to the bottom, 
\par Frills and bows for ey\rquote ryone to see. 
\par There were shoutin\rquote , screamin\rquote , ey\rquote rythin\rquote  were rife, 
\par I seen some funny things I\rquote ve never seen in all mi life, 
\par I even seen some things I\rquote ve never seen upon the wife, 
\par At the Rawtenstall Annual Fair. 
\par 
\par \ldblquote Roll up, roll up, see the tattoed lady, 
\par See the lovely lady with the pictures on her skin.\rdblquote  
\par In went the lads and they began to cheer, 
\par For tattooed on \lquote er back were all the towns o\rquote  Lancashire. 
\par There were Odsall Bottom, Manchester City, 
\par The town hall standin\rquote  in the square. 
\par There were Oldham, Bolton, Ashton-under-Lyne, 
\par Coal pit up at Moston, it were lookin\rquote  mighty fine, 
\par 'Til some bugger shouted \ldblquote Daddy, don\rquote t go down the mine!\rdblquote , 
\par At the Rawtenstall Annual Fair. 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\li1440\sl-259\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \b\fs28\lang2057 {\page Seven Nights Drunk 
\par }\pard\plain \li1440\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057 {
\par When I came home on Monday night, as drunk as drunk could be 
\par I saw a horse outside the door, where my old horse should be 
\par So I called my wife, }{\i (Shout: }{Hey wife!) 
\par And I said to her, would you kindly tell to me 
\par Who owns that horse outside my}{\b  }{door, where my old horse should be? 
\par 
\par }{\i Oh, you\rquote re drunk, you\rquote re drunk, you silly old fool, 
\par So drunk you cannot see? 
\par That\rquote s a lovely milking cow that my mother sent to me. 
\par Well, it\rquote s many a day I\rquote ve travelled, a hundred miles or more, 
\par But a saddle on a milking cow I\rquote ve never seen before! 
\par }{
\par When I came home on Tuesday night, as drunk as drunk could be 
\par I saw a coat behind the door, where my old coat should be 
\par So I called my wife, }{\i (Shout: }{Hey wife!) 
\par And I said to her, would you kindly tell to me 
\par Who owns that coat outside my door, where my old coat should be? 
\par 
\par }{\i Oh, you\rquote re drunk, you\rquote re drunk, you silly old fool, 
\par So drunk you cannot see? 
\par That\rquote s a lovely blanket that my mother sent to me. 
\par Well, it\rquote s many a day I\rquote ve travelled, a hundred miles or more, 
\par But buttons on a blanket I\rquote ve never seen before! 
\par }{
\par When I came home on Wednesday night, as drunk as drunk could be 
\par I saw a pipe upon the chair, where my old pipe should be 
\par So I called my wife, }{\i (Shout: }{Hey wife!) 
\par And I said to her, would you kindly tell to me 
\par Who owns that pipe upon the chair, where my old pipe should be? 
\par 
\par }{\i Oh, you\rquote re drunk, you\rquote re drunk, you silly old fool, 
\par So drunk you cannot see? 
\par That\rquote s a lovely tin whistle that my mother sent to me. 
\par Well, it\rquote s many a day I\rquote ve travelled, a hundred miles or more, 
\par But baccy in a whistle I\rquote ve never seen before! 
\par }{
\par When I came home on Thursday night, as drunk as drunk could be 
\par I saw two boots beneath the bed, where my old boots should be 
\par So I called my wife, }{\i (Shout: }{Hey wife!) 
\par And I said to her, would you kindly tell to me 
\par Who owns those boots beneath the bed, where my old boots should be? 
\par 
\par }{\i Oh, you\rquote re drunk, you\rquote re drunk, you silly old fool, 
\par So drunk you cannot see? 
\par That\rquote s two lovely plant pots that my mother sent to me. 
\par Well, it\rquote s many a day I\rquote ve travelled, a hundred miles or more, 
\par But laces in a plant pot I\rquote ve never seen before! 
\par }{
\par When I came home on Friday night, as drunk as drunk could be 
\par I saw a head upon the bed, where my old head should be 
\par So I called my wife, }{\i (Shout: }{Hey wife!) 
\par And I said to her, would you kindly tell to me 
\par Who owns a head upon the bed, where my old head should be? 
\par \page }{\i Oh, you\rquote re drunk, you\rquote re drunk, you silly old fool, 
\par So drunk you cannot see? 
\par That\rquote s a lovely baby boy my mother sent to me. 
\par Well, it\rquote s many a day I\rquote ve travelled, a hundred miles or more, 
\par But whiskers on a baby boy I\rquote ve never seen before! 
\par }{
\par When I came home on Saturday night, as drunk as drunk could be 
\par I saw a rise beneath the sheets, where my old rise should be 
\par So I called my wife, }{\i (Shout: }{Hey wife!) 
\par And I said to her, would you kindly tell to me 
\par Who owns that rise beneath the sheets, where my old rise should be? 
\par 
\par }{\i Oh, you\rquote re drunk, you\rquote re drunk, you silly old fool, 
\par So drunk you cannot see? 
\par It\rquote s nothing but a shillelagh my mother sent to me. 
\par Well, it\rquote s many a day I\rquote ve travelled, a hundred miles or more, 
\par But knackers on a shillelagh I\rquote ve never seen before! 
\par }{
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\li1440\sl-259\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \b\fs28\lang2057 {Whisky in the jar 
\par }\pard\plain \li1440\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057 {
\par As I was going over Kilgarry Mountain 
\par I met with Captain Farrell and his money he was countin\rquote  
\par I first}{\b  }{produced me pistol, then I drew me sabre, 
\par Saying Stand and Deliver for I am your bold deceiver. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus: 
\par With a ring-o-ma-doo-do-ma-da 
\par What for me daddy-o 
\par What for me daddy-o 
\par There\rquote s whiskey in the jar. 
\par }{
\par He counted out his money and it made a pretty penny 
\par I loaded up and took it home and gave it to my Jenny 
\par She swore that she loved me, that she never would deceive me 
\par But the devil\rquote s in the women and they always lie so easy. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par I woke next morning early \lquote tween the hours of six and seven 
\par And the guards were standing round the bed in numbers odd and even 
\par I flew to my pistols but alas I was mistaken 
\par For Jenny\rquote d wet the powder and a prisoner I was taken.
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }{
\par They threw me into Sligo Jail with neither judge not writing 
\par For robbing Captain Farrell as he crossed Kilgarry Mountain 
\par But they didn\rquote t take my fists so I knocked the jailer down 
\par And bid a distant farewell to the judge in Sligo Town. 
\par 
\par }{\i Chorus 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
